  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The common thread: pay for value you'll actually use</h2>
    <p>Annual fees, protection plans, and service charges all share one trick: they are priced against what you might use, while the seller expects you to use less. The tools in this section replace the advertised value with your realistic value.</p>

    <h3>Premium credit cards</h3>
    <p>A card with a $395 fee that advertises $580 in credits only comes out ahead if you would have spent that money anyway. The <a href="../../calculators/credit-card-annual-fee.html">credit card annual fee calculator</a> counts each credit at the share you'll really use, values points at your actual redemption rate, and compares the result with a no-fee card. In its default example, the premium card's advantage disappears if you use only the main travel credit, or if your points are worth less than 0.89¢ each.</p>

    <h3>Extended warranties</h3>
    <p>Retailers sell protection plans because, on average, they collect more than they pay out. The <a href="../../calculators/extended-warranty.html">extended warranty calculator</a> compares a plan's price with the expected cost of repairs, using a failure rate and repair cost you can adjust, and finds the failure odds at which the plan breaks even. 2 things often make a plan worth even less: the manufacturer's warranty already covers the first year or more, and many credit cards extend that warranty at no extra cost. A plan can still make sense if a single repair would be a financial hardship.</p>

    <h3>Tips and split bills</h3>
    <p>The <a href="../../calculators/tip.html">tip calculator</a> handles the everyday version: what to leave, whether to tip on the pre-tax amount, and how to split it. Watch for automatic gratuities on large-party checks and for suggested-tip screens that calculate on the after-tax total.</p>

    <h2>A quick test before paying for any add-on</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>What would you realistically get from it in a year, in dollars?</li>
      <li>What does the cheapest alternative cost, including doing without it?</li>
      <li>Could you absorb the worst case yourself? If yes, you are paying for convenience, not protection.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Putting the amount you would have spent on add-ons into a savings account builds your own coverage, which you keep if nothing breaks.</p>
  </div>
</section>
